 for bestpractices and # Agent Console

## Overview
Create an interactive Agent Console within LifeOps that serves as the operational cockpit for inspecting and controlling individual GPT-5 powered agents. The console provides deep visibility into an agent’s configuration, permissions, memory snapshots, and run simulations. It integrates with a Scoped Shared Memory & Vector DB, a Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer, and an Agent SDK & Tooling to enable safe, auditable, and scalable agent management. The UI emphasizes dark-mode ergonomics, modular card-based layouts, traceability of agent-to-agent communications, and one-click actions for control and throughput tuning. All data workflows must be robust against null/undefined values and guarded against runtime errors, per the runtime safety guidelines.

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- The Agent Console is an operational cockpit dedicated to a single agent (identifier, name, or UUID). It exposes:
  - Agent configuration: role, prompts/templates, allowed tools, memory access permissions, cost controls, rate limits, and safety constraints.
  - Permissions and access scope: who/what can invoke the agent, resource scopes, and approval requirements.
  - Memory snapshots: read/write access to scoped memory, TTLs, and recent vector DB-backed memory entries; includes filters by scope, TTL, and access rules.
  - Run simulation: compose an input payload or prompt, run a simulated interaction, and visualize the trace of messages, including agent-to-agent handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus points.
  - Controls to pause/resume automation and adjust automation level or risk posture.
- Connected features:
  - Scoped Shared Memory & Vector DB: read/write scoped memory with TTLs, backed by a vector DB for retrieval and a relational store for metadata. The UI should show memory keys, scopes, last writes, TTLs, and access controls; provide actions to write/read, and demonstrate permission checks.
  - Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer: provide a live or simulated view of how this agent interacts with others in a run, including handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus events.
  - Agent SDK & Tooling: present and edit the configured tools, prompts/templates, and cost controls with safe defaults and validation.
- UI elements and visual guidance:
  - Agent List: compact rows showing status (online/offline), last activity timestamp, and quick actions (pause/resume, run simulation).
  - Agent Detail A: Tabs or sections for Config, Permissions, Memory, and Simulation.
  - Memory Snapshot Viewer: filterable, paginated list of memory entries with vector embeddings, TTL, scope, and access control metadata; include add/edit/delete operations guarded by permissions.
  - Run Simulation Panel: input area for sim prompts, run controls (start/stop/pause), and a live trace panel showing messages with arrows, timestamps, sender/receiver, and verdicts.
  - Trace Highlights: visual timeline correlating to Agent Trace Viewer, with markers for handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus steps.
  - Action Bar: contextual actions (Save Config, Export run artifacts, Generate Diff/Artifacts) with safe mode toggles.
- API integrations:
  - Read/write operations against the Scoped Shared Memory API, including TTL-aware reads and scoped ACL checks.
  - Agent orchestration endpoints to fetch run traces, agent handoffs, and consensus points for a given run.
  - SDK management endpoints for tools, prompts, and memory access policies.
  - Simulation microservice to run a local, deterministic simulation with a given payload and return trace data.

## Components to Build
- Agent Console Dashboard Card
  - Type: React component (or framework-equivalent)
  - Props: agentId, initialState
  - Features: status badge, lastActivity, quick action buttons
- Agent Detail Section
  - Tabs: Config, Permissions, Memory, Simulation
  - Each tab renders dedicated subcomponents
- Memory Snapshot Viewer
  - Table/list of memory entries with: key, value (masked/expanded as needed), scope, ttl, lastModified, permissions
  - Filters: scope, ttl, permission level; search; sort options
  - Actions: read, write, delete (guarded by ACL)
- Run Simulation Panel
  - Prompt template editor
  - Input payload builder
  - Start/Pause/Resume controls
  - Live Trace Panel: list/graphical timeline of messages
- Agent Trace Visualizer (integration with Agent Trace Viewer)
  - Timeline view per run with lanes for agents
  - Markers for handoffs, negotiations, alerts, consensus
- Orchestration & Tools Inspector
  - Overview of neighboring agents, tool usage, and handoff rules
  - Real-time indicators of active negotiations
- Master Utilities
  - Config Save/Export, Artifact Dump, Replay/Diff, Re-run options
- API Client Modules
  - MemoryAPI, OrchestrationAPI, SDKToolingAPI, SimulationAPI
- UI Elements
  - Cards with consistent elevation, hover states, and action footers
  - Left sidebar navigation, top search, and action bar

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Use a 12-column responsive grid with 24–32px gutters.
- Initialize arrays with safe defaults: useState<Type[]>([]) for lists (e.g., memory entries, traces, tools).
- Guard all array operations:
  - (items ?? []).map(...)
  - Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
- Memory and API data handling:
  - Always validate API responses: const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
  - Access nested objects with optional chaining: resp?.agent?.config?.tooling
- UI components:
  - Card components with border radius 12–16px, subtle shadows, hover elevation
  - Tabs with accessible keyboard navigation
  - Tables/lists with sortable, filterable columns
  - Buttons: primary blue (#4F8CFF), secondary gray; focus outlines in accent color
  - Forms: large, rounded inputs, accessible labels, high-contrast placeholders
- State management:
  - Localized component state for the Agent Console with useState and useEffect
  - Optional: lightweight global state for current selected agent if needed
- Data visualization:
  - Simple charts for traces and memory trends using preferred chart library; ensure dark-theme compatibility and accessible contrast
- Accessibility:
  - ARIA labels for interactive controls
  - Keyboard navigable controls
  - High-contrast text/icons
- Performance:
  - Debounce search/filter inputs
  - Lazy load long lists where feasible

### Backend
- API Endpoints (to be implemented or mocked for integration):
  - GET /agents/{agentId} - fetch agent profile and config
  - GET /agents/{agentId}/memory?scope=&limit=&offset= - fetch memory entries (guard nulls: data ?? [])
  - POST /agents/{agentId}/memory - write memory entry with ttl and scope
  - GET /agents/{agentId}/trace?runId=&limit= - fetch trace for a run
  - POST /agents/{agentId}/simulate - run simulation with payload; return trace
  - GET /orchestrator/run/{runId}/summary - fetch run summary and handoffs
  - GET /tools - fetch allowed tools and templates
  - PATCH /agents/{agentId}/config - update agent config
  - PATCH /agents/{agentId}/permissions - update permissions
- Database schema (relational + vector metadata):
  - agents: id, name, status, last_activity, automation_level, cost_control
  - memory_entries: id, agent_id, key, value, scope, ttl, created_at, updated_at, access_control
  - memory_vectors: id, memory_id, vector (VECTOR), metadata
  - runs: id, agent_id, status, started_at, ended_at, trace_id, summary
  - traces: id, run_id, sender_id, receiver_id, message, timestamp, type (handoff/negotiation/alert/consensus)
  - tools: id, name, version, config_schema
  - permissions: id, agent_id, subjects, allowed_actions
- Validation and safety:
  - Validate inputs; reject invalid prompts or tools
  - Enforce TTLs and ACLs before reads/writes
  - Ensure all reads that populate UI use data ?? [] or Array.isArray guards

### Integration
- Client-Server contracts:
  - All responses must normalize to consistent shapes; guard against missing fields
  - Use data normalization layers to convert API responses into UI models
- Data flow:
  - Agent Console fetches agent profile, memory, and tools on load
  - User performs memory write; optimistic UI with rollback on failure
  - User runs simulation; UI shows live trace as simulation progresses
  - Trace viewer ties to runId to render Agent Trace Viewer integration
- Error handling:
  - Centralized error toast/notification system
  - Retry logic with exponential backoff for transient API failures
  - Graceful handling when data is missing or null

## User Experience Flow
1. User opens the LifeOps Master Dashboard and selects an agent to inspect.
2. Agent Console loads with:
   - Summary header: agent name, status, last activity, automation level
   - Tabs: Config, Permissions, Memory, Simulation
3. In Config:
   - View current prompts/templates, allowed tools, and cost controls
   - Edit fields with inline validation
   - Save changes; show audit trail
4. In Permissions:
   - View and modify who/what can invoke the agent
   - Set access scopes and approval requirements
   - Save with success confirmation
5. In Memory:
   - View memory entries by scope; filter by TTL; search by key
   - Read or write memory entries; TTL and scope enforced
   - Snapshot viewer with pagination
6. In Simulation:
   - Compose or select a prompt template
   - Provide input payload; start simulation
   - See live trace: messages, handoffs, negotiations, alerts, consensus
   - Pause/resume and adjust automation level during or after run
7. Trace integration:
   - Open Agent Trace Viewer for the selected run to inspect the sequence visually
8. Master actions:
   - Export run artifacts, replay run, or create diffs
   - Access to related tools and run history in the Orchestration Layer
9. All actions are logged, auditable, and reversible with a full run artifact

## Technical Specifications

Data Models: 
- Agent
  - id: string
  - name: string
  - status: 'online' | 'offline' | 'paused' | 'error'
  - last_activity: string (ISO timestamp)
  - automation_level: 'manual' | 'suggest-only' | 'conditional-auto' | 'bounded-autopilot'
  - cost_control: object (limits, currency, spent)
- MemoryEntry
  - id: string
  - agent_id: string
  - key: string
  - value: any
  - scope: string
  - ttl: number | null
  - created_at: string
  - updated_at: string
  - access_control: object
- Run
  - id: string
  - agent_id: string
  - status: string
  - started_at: string
  - ended_at: string | null
  - trace_id: string
  - summary: string
- Trace
  - id: string
  - run_id: string
  - sender_id: string
  - receiver_id: string
  - message: string
  - timestamp: string
  - type: 'handoff'|'negotiation'|'alert'|'consensus'
- Tool
  - id: string
  - name: string
  - version: string
  - config_schema: object

API Endpoints (Routes and methods):
- GET /agents/{agentId}
- PATCH /agents/{agentId}/config
- PATCH /agents/{agentId}/permissions
- GET /agents/{agentId}/memory?scope=&limit=&offset=
- POST /agents/{agentId}/memory
- GET /agents/{agentId}/trace?runId=&limit=
- POST /agents/{agentId}/simulate
- GET /orchestrator/run/{runId}/summary
- GET /tools
- GET /agents/{agentId}/memory/summary
- POST /agents/{agentId}/memory/query (read with ACL)

Security:
- Authentication: OAuth2/OIDC or JWT per LifeOps standards
- Authorization: Role-based access control with per-agent resource scopes
- Data handling: Validate all inputs; enforce permissions on every read/write
- Audit: Log all changes with user, timestamp, and rationale
- Rate limiting and quotas on simulation and memory operations

Validation:
- All inputs validated server-side with schema checks
- Client-side form validation with real-time feedback
- Use "data ?? []" gating for all array results from API calls
- Confirm that arrays are real arrays before mapping: Array.isArray(data) ? data.map(...) : []

Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Agent Console loads with correctly structured agent data and no runtime errors
- [ ] Memory viewer shows scoped entries; writing memory enforces TTL and scope
- [ ] Simulation run produces a live trace with no null/undefined crash points
- [ ] Trace Viewer accurately reflects handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus
- [ ] All data fetches guard against null data and undefined values using data ?? [] or Array.isArray checks
- [ ] UI adheres to the dark-mode-first design system, with accessible controls and responsive layout
- [ ] Security and validation pass: API responses normalized, inputs validated, permissions enforced

UI/UX Guidelines
Apply LifeOps design system consistently:
- Focus on modular, card-based sections with clear visual hierarchy
- Use the provided color palette for background, accents, text, borders, and status indicators
- Cards with rounded corners 12–16px; subtle inner shadows and elevation
- Sidebar navigation with collapsible groups; top bar search and actions
- Data visualizations with neon accent lines on dark backgrounds; avoid heavy gradients
- Interactive elements with smooth hover transitions; focus outlines in accent color
- Accessibility: keyboard navigable, ARIA attributes, high-contrast text

Visual Style
- Colors, typography, and layout as described in the Visual Style section
- No gradients; prefer flat, solid blocks with tonal variation

Mandatory Coding Standards — Runtime Safety

CRITICAL: Follow these rules in ALL generated code to prevent runtime crashes.

1. Supabase query results: Always use nullish coalescing — const items = data ?? []. Supabase returns null when there are no rows.
2. Array methods: Never call on a value that could be null, undefined, or non-array. Guard:
   - (items ?? []).map(...) or
   - Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
3. React useState for arrays/objects: Initialize with correct type — useState<Type[]>([]) for arrays
4. API response shapes: Always validate — const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
5. Optional chaining: Use obj?.property?.nested when accessing nested API/database results
6. Destructuring with defaults: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}

Notes
- Ensure all components and data interactions are resilient to missing fields.
- Provide clear error messages and fallbacks in the UI when data is missing or access is denied.
- Include unit tests or integration tests where applicable to validate null-safety patterns.

This prompt intends to guide an AI development tool to generate a complete, production-ready Agent Console with the features described, while enforcing strict runtime safety and UI/UX consistency. for style.

CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
- You MUST follow ALL design principles and patterns specified in the Design Reference above
- Do NOT deviate from the design specifications without explicit user permission
- Implement ALL visual design elements (colors, typography, spacing, shadows, animations) as specified
- Follow the layout patterns and component designs exactly as documented
- Ensure all interactions and micro-animations match the reference specifications

CRITICAL TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- React 18.3.1, React Router 6.30.1
- Tailwind CSS v3 with tailwind.config.js and custom keyframes
- @tailwindcss/typography plugin for rich text content
- Tailwind CSS animations (NOT Motion library or framer-motion)
- Sonner for toasts
- Recharts for data visualization
- Native fetch() with API utilities in src/lib/api.ts
- Design system with CSS custom properties
- Use RGB color values for theming
- When Supabase is configured: use Edge Functions for LLM and server-only logic; never expose API keys in the client

